  <commands>
    <!--
      Scoped test command: runs only the tests covering the TDD code. This
      keeps per-mutant runtime under 11s and ensures the mutation score is
      attributable to the new code (not noisy co-test failures from the
      rest of the repo).

      Why a wrapper script and not `bash -c "rm -rf ...; dart test ..."`
      inline: the `mutation_test` package (v1.8.0) parses <command> text by
      splitting on whitespace (see configuration.dart `_addCommand`); it
      does NOT honor shell quotes. Inline bash -c with quoted redirect
      strings tokenizes as ["bash", "-c", "\"rm", "-rf", ...] and bash
      errors with "unexpected EOF while looking for matching quote".
      Putting the logic in `tools/run-tdd-tests.sh` and invoking
      `bash tools/run-tdd-tests.sh` keeps args tokenizable on whitespace
      while preserving shell quoting inside the script.

      The wrapper script:
        - cleans leftover $TMPDIR/dart_test.kernel.* dirs from prior
          mutants (the dart test runner leaks them; rootfs fills after
          ~6 mutants and corrupts the audit by miscategorizing mutants
          as "NotCovered")
        - deliberately does NOT delete `.dart_tool/test/incremental_kernel*`
          (the persistent incremental-compile cache; removing it forces
          every mutant to recompile ~133 transitive dills from scratch)
        - runs `dart test -j 1` to avoid the parallel-test CWD-cascade
          flake (issue #506)

      See `tools/run-tdd-tests.sh` for the full implementation.
    -->
    <command group="test" expected-return="0" working-directory=".">bash tools/run-tdd-tests.sh</command>
  </commands>
</mutations>
